+# 0013. Parse the flags with a hand-written parser
+
+Date: 2026-10-22
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+This is a product record ([ADR-0010](0010-record-product-architecture-decisions.md)).
+The command is `tally [-l] [-w] [-c] [file...]`; combined flags such as `-lw` mean
+`-l -w`, the name `-` is a file, and an unknown flag or wrong usage prints a usage
+message on standard error and exits with 2 ([REQ-002 to REQ-005](../prd/PRD-0001-tally.md),
+Q-002). The counts print in the fixed order lines, words, characters, whatever the
+order of the flags (Inv-1 of [`guardrails.md`](../guardrails.md)). Only the
+standard library may be used (NFR-019). Three options were filtered:
+`hand-written-parser`, `stdlib-flag-package` and `third-party-getopt`.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will parse the command line with a parser written in the package `main`. It
+reads each argument that starts with `-` and has more letters as a set of the flags
+`l`, `w` and `c`, so `-lw` sets two flags; it reads the argument `-` alone as a file
+name; it reports any other letter, or any other wrong usage, as a usage error with
+the exit code 2. The flags only select counts; the order of the printed counts is
+fixed apart from them. The architecture is
+[SPEC-0002](../spec/SPEC-0002-tally-architecture.md).
+
+We reject `stdlib-flag-package`, the package `flag` of the standard library: it
+fails REQ-003, because it does not read `-lw` as `-l -w`, and Inv-1. We reject
+`third-party-getopt`: it fails NFR-019 and fact F-0003#2, because it is a
+third-party dependency.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The parser is small and is the command's own code to test, every combination of
+  flags included.
+- The usage message and its wording are the parser's to own.
