+# 0014. Stream each file through a fixed buffer
+
+Date: 2026-10-22
+
+## Status
+
+Accepted
+
+## Context
+
+This is a product record ([ADR-0010](0010-record-product-architecture-decisions.md)).
+The peak heap while tally counts a file of 1 GiB is at most 64 MiB, sampled every 10
+milliseconds ([NFR and success metrics of PRD-0001](../prd/PRD-0001-tally.md),
+F-0003#24, Q-009), and a file of 10 MiB is counted in at most 2 seconds (Q-008). Two
+options were filtered: `stream-fixed-buffer` and `read-whole-file`.
+
+## Decision
+
+We will read each file and standard input in pieces, through a buffer of fixed size
+that is reused for the whole source, and count each piece as it arrives. The heap
+the reading needs then does not grow with the size of the file. A code point split
+across two pieces is carried to the next piece, never counted twice nor lost. The
+architecture is [SPEC-0002](../spec/SPEC-0002-tally-architecture.md).
+
+We reject `read-whole-file`, which reads the whole file into memory before counting:
+it fails the peak-heap criterion (draft R-026), because a file of 1 GiB would need
+at least 1 GiB of heap.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- The memory a count uses is bounded by the buffer, whatever the size of the file.
+- The size of the buffer is left unfrozen; the time and memory tests of the success
+  metrics judge it.
