+# SPEC-0002. The architecture of tally
+
+Date: 2026-10-22
+
+This document states how tally is built, to meet the behaviour that
+[SPEC-0001](SPEC-0001-tally.md) specifies for each requirement of
+[PRD-0001](../prd/PRD-0001-tally.md). It records seven decisions. For each one,
+options were filtered against the requirements, the facts, the invariants of
+[`guardrails.md`](../guardrails.md) and the records ADR-0001 to ADR-0009. A decision
+with one survivor is decided here and in its own ADR. A decision with more than one
+survivor is written as a section "Decision pending the bet": it lists the survivors
+and decides nothing, and the bet picks one. It builds nothing.
+
+## Decisions at a glance
+
+| Decision | Survivors of the filter | State | Record |
+| -------- | ----------------------- | ----- | ------ |
+| Build tool | `go-build-only` | Decided | [ADR-0011](../adr/0011-build-with-go-build-only.md) |
+| Module layout | `root-main-count-subpackage`, `cmd-tally-count-subpackage` | Pending the bet | [below](#module-layout) |
+| Count interface | `three-functions-teed-reader` | Decided | [ADR-0012](../adr/0012-count-with-three-functions-over-a-teed-reader.md) |
+| Flag parsing | `hand-written-parser` | Decided | [ADR-0013](../adr/0013-parse-flags-by-hand.md) |
+| Read strategy | `stream-fixed-buffer` | Decided | [ADR-0014](../adr/0014-stream-through-a-fixed-buffer.md) |
+| UTF-8 validation | `validate-in-stream-print-after-eof` | Decided | [ADR-0015](../adr/0015-validate-utf-8-in-the-stream.md) |
+| White-space class | `unicode-isspace`, `unicode-white-space-table` | Pending the bet | [below](#white-space-class) |
+
+The product records follow [ADR-0010](../adr/0010-record-product-architecture-decisions.md).
+
+## Build tool
+
+Decided: `go-build-only`. The Go toolchain alone builds and tests tally — `go build`
+for the binary, `go test` for the tests — with no build file of another tool. The
+module has no `require` directive and imports the standard library only.
+Rejected: `make-wrapper` fails NFR-020 and F-0003#3. Serves NFR-018, NFR-019 and
+NFR-020. ([ADR-0011](../adr/0011-build-with-go-build-only.md))
+
+## Module layout
+
+One Go module holds the package `main` of the command and a separate package
+`count` (NFR-021, Q-010). Where the package `main` sits in the module is not decided.
+Rejected: `single-main-package`, which puts the counting code in the package `main`,
+fails NFR-021 and Inv-7.
+
+### Decision pending the bet
+
+Two options survive the filter, and this document chooses neither:
+
+- `root-main-count-subpackage` — the package `main` at the root of the module, and
+  the package `count` in a subdirectory.
+- `cmd-tally-count-subpackage` — the package `main` under `cmd/tally`, and the
+  package `count` in a subdirectory.
+
+## Count interface
+
+Decided: `three-functions-teed-reader`. The package `count` exports three
+functions, one each for the lines (REQ-009), the words (REQ-010) and the characters
+(REQ-011), each over an `io.Reader`. The command reads each source once and feeds
+the same bytes to the three counts through a teed reader, so standard input, which
+cannot be read twice, is counted in one pass. Rejected:
+`three-functions-reread-source` fails REQ-008; `single-combined-function` fails
+NFR-021. Serves REQ-001, REQ-008 to REQ-011 and NFR-021.
+([ADR-0012](../adr/0012-count-with-three-functions-over-a-teed-reader.md))
+
+## Flag parsing
+
+Decided: `hand-written-parser`. The package `main` parses the command line itself:
+an argument that starts with `-` and has more letters is a set of the flags `l`, `w`
+and `c`, so `-lw` means `-l -w`; the argument `-` alone is a file name; any other
+letter or other wrong usage prints a usage message on standard error, counts
+nothing and exits with 2. With no flag, the three counts are selected; the counts
+always print in the order lines, words, characters. Rejected: `stdlib-flag-package`
+fails REQ-003 and Inv-1; `third-party-getopt` fails NFR-019 and F-0003#2. Serves
+REQ-002 to REQ-007 and REQ-012. ([ADR-0013](../adr/0013-parse-flags-by-hand.md))
+
+## Read strategy
+
+Decided: `stream-fixed-buffer`. Each file and standard input is read in pieces
+through one buffer of fixed size, and each piece is counted as it arrives; a code
+point split across two pieces is carried to the next. The heap the reading needs
+does not grow with the file. The size of the buffer is left unfrozen. Rejected:
+`read-whole-file` fails the peak-heap criterion (draft R-026). Serves the success
+metrics of time and peak heap. ([ADR-0014](../adr/0014-stream-through-a-fixed-buffer.md))
+
+## UTF-8 validation
+
+Decided: `validate-in-stream-print-after-eof`. The bytes are checked for valid
+UTF-8 as they stream through the buffer, and a file's row is printed only after its
+end is reached with no invalid sequence. On an invalid sequence, tally prints a
+message with the file name on standard error, prints no row, adds nothing to the
+total, goes on with the next file and exits with 1 at the end; rows already printed
+stay. A file that cannot be opened or read, a directory included, takes the same
+path with its reason (REQ-014, REQ-015). Rejected: `print-row-while-reading` fails
+Inv-3; `replace-invalid-with-fffd` fails NFR-023, F-0003#11 and Inv-3. Serves
+REQ-013 to REQ-017 and NFR-023.
+([ADR-0015](../adr/0015-validate-utf-8-in-the-stream.md))
+
+## White-space class
+
+A word is a run of code points that are not white space, and white space is every
+code point with the Unicode property `White_Space` (REQ-010). How the package
+`count` tests that property is not decided. Rejected: `ascii-whitespace-set`, which
+knows only the ASCII white space, fails REQ-010.
+
+### Decision pending the bet
+
+Two options survive the filter, and this document chooses neither:
+
+- `unicode-isspace` — test each code point with `unicode.IsSpace` of the standard
+  library.
+- `unicode-white-space-table` — test each code point against the table
+  `unicode.White_Space` of the standard library.
+
+## How the parts meet
+
+The package `main` parses the arguments (Flag parsing), then opens each named file
+in order, or reads standard input when no name is given. It streams each source
+through the fixed buffer (Read strategy), validating UTF-8 as it goes (UTF-8
+validation), and feeds the bytes once to the three count functions of the package
+`count` (Count interface), which use the white-space test of the White-space class.
+After a source ends valid, the package `main` prints its row with the selected
+counts in the fixed order, adds the counts to the total, and after the last source
+prints the total row when two names or more were given. It ends with the exit code
+2 on a usage error, 1 when any source failed, and 0 otherwise.
